    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Building Credit in the US as a Non-Citizen and What It Means for Your Mortgage</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've been living and working in the United States for several years, paying rent on time every month, and managing your finances responsibly, but have little or no US credit history, you're not alone and you're not disqualified from buying a home. The US credit system wasn't designed with immigrants in mind, and the gap between how well you manage money and what a credit report reflects is a structural problem, not a reflection of your financial reliability.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why US Credit History Matters More Than Credit History From Anywhere Else</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">When a US mortgage lender evaluates your creditworthiness, they are almost exclusively working within the US credit reporting system. A strong credit history from your home country, years of on-time payments to financial institutions in another currency and jurisdiction, carries no direct weight in a US mortgage application. The lender can't pull it, can't verify it through standard systems, and in most cases can't formally incorporate it into their underwriting decision. You may have an excellent financial track record spanning decades, and a US lender reviewing your application will see a file with no credit history at all.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why Starting From Zero Takes Longer Than Most People Expect</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's the challenge that catches non-citizens off guard when they first start thinking about buying a home. Building a meaningful US credit profile from scratch takes time, and the timeline is longer than most people anticipate. A credit score requires at least six months of account activity to generate. A mortgage-worthy credit profile, one that qualifies you for reasonable pricing on an ITIN loan, generally requires 12 to 24 months of consistent, on-time payment history across multiple account types.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If you haven't started building US credit yet and you want to buy a home in the next two to three years, the time to start is now, not when you're ready to apply.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How to Build US Credit as a Non-Citizen</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The most accessible starting point for most non-citizens is a secured credit card. A secured card requires a deposit that becomes your credit limit, which means the issuer takes no risk and approval doesn't depend on an existing credit history. Using the card for small, regular purchases and paying the balance in full every month builds payment history, which is the single largest factor in your credit score.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">After six to twelve months of secured card history, you may be able to qualify for an unsecured card or a credit-builder loan, both of which add additional account types to your file and continue building history. The goal is to have at least two to three active accounts with a consistent on-time payment record before you apply for a mortgage.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For borrowers who don't yet have enough US credit history to generate a score, some ITIN lenders accept alternative credit documentation. Twelve months of cancelled rent checks or a letter from your landlord confirming on-time payment history, utility bills paid consistently over a year, phone plan payment records, and similar documentation can substitute for a traditional credit report with lenders who have built their programs to accommodate thin-file borrowers.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Open a secured credit card as soon as possible if you don't already have US credit.</strong> Use it monthly for small purchases and pay the full balance every month. This builds the payment history your credit score depends on.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not open multiple accounts at once.</strong> Each new application creates a hard inquiry and lowers the average age of your accounts. Open one account, establish a history, then add a second.</li>
      <li><strong>Set up automatic payments on every account.</strong> A single missed payment can significantly damage a young credit file. Automating payments protects your history even when life gets busy.</li>
      <li><strong>Check your credit report after six months.</strong> Confirm that your accounts are reporting correctly and that your payment history is being captured accurately. Errors on young credit files can suppress a score before it has a chance to grow.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask your ITIN lender what alternative credit documentation they accept if your US credit history is limited.</strong> Know what they'll work with before you decide to delay your purchase while you build more history.</li>
      <li><strong>Give yourself 12 to 24 months of credit-building time before your target purchase date.</strong> The earlier you start, the stronger your profile will be when you apply.</li>
    </ol>

